gm-method/rendering lesson - two distinct 'thing disappears' culling bugs in a three InstancedMesh2 + LOD consumer, both root-caused by process-of-elimination:

(1) INSTANCED-MESH LOD PARTIAL BOUNDING SPHERE = cull-on-approach. When a per-instance frustum-culled InstancedMesh (perObjectFrustumCulled) uses addLOD with SIMPLIFIED geometries, each LOD geo gets its OWN computeBoundingSphere = a PARTIAL sphere (e.g. trunk-only for a branch mesh, canopy-only for a leaf mesh). The per-instance cull tests that partial sphere transformed by the instance matrix, so as the camera APPROACHES and looks up/around, the partial sphere leaves the frustum and the whole instance culls while still on-screen ('trees cull when getting closer'). FIX: stamp the SHARED whole-object bounding sphere onto EVERY LOD geometry (not just LOD0) before addLOD, so all LODs cull as one unit. The base/LOD0 sphere alone is insufficient - the active LOD at the relevant distance is LOD1/LOD2. Witness: place the camera a few metres from an instance looking up, count its draw calls (mesh.visible toggle delta) - it must stay >0.

(2) LOCAL PLAYER DISTANCE-CULL = intermittent vanish. A per-frame distance cull loop (m.visible = dist<=R) that iterates ALL player meshes WITHOUT exempting the LOCAL player flips it invisible on transient frames where the local mesh position briefly spikes far from the camera (respawn/teleport, netcode reconciliation snap, camera-mode transition before the follow re-converges). The local player is the camera subject and must ALWAYS be visible - exempt id===localId from any distance cull; only distance-cull REMOTE players. Witness: force the local mesh far from the camera, run the cull logic, assert it stays visible.

GENERAL: a SkinnedMesh/instanced/LOD 'disappears in some spots' is almost always a frustum-cull testing a bounding volume that doesn't track the ACTUAL on-screen geometry (bind-pose sphere for skinned, partial-LOD sphere for LOD, or a wrongly-distance-gated local subject). The fix is either frustumCulled=false (always-near single meshes like the local avatar) or forcing the correct whole-object bounding volume onto every cull-tested geometry. Audit sibling systems (rocks/grass) for the same pattern - here only the LOD'd one (vegetation) had it; BatchedMesh + single-geo systems were immune.